  "final_report": "[stop_reason: hop_cap_reached | hops used: 4/4]\n\nAnalyst summary:\n**What the supply\u2011graph tells us**\n\nThe downstream traversal from **Formosa\u202fPetrochemical** (max\u2011tier\u202f=\u202f2, mode\u202f=\u202fdownstream) returned a list of 98 \u201cenriched\u201d companies that are either direct customers of Formosa or alternative suppliers for the same products.  \n\n- **Tier\u202f1 (direct downstream customers / immediate alternatives)** \u2013 13 firms were identified.  \n- **Tier\u202f2 (customers of Tier\u20111 firms, or second\u2011order alternatives)** \u2013 45 firms were identified.\n\nOnly the companies that are flagged \u201c**HAS a filing on record**\u201d can be examined with `get_supplier_info`. Those are the only ones for which we have any documentary evidence; all others return nothing from a filing request and would require a separate corpus search, which is not possible within the remaining turn budget.\n\nBelow is the subset of companies that actually have SEC\u2011type filings and therefore constitute the **significant, verifiable set**.  \n\n| Tier | Company (has filing) | Evidence retrieved (tool used) | What the evidence shows about the Formosa disruption |\n|------|-----------------------|--------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------|\n| 1    | **Shell** (Royal Dutch Shell plc) | `get_supplier_info` \u2013 20\u2011F filed\u202f15\u202fMar\u202f2018 (\u2248\u202f388\u202fdays before the reported disruption) | The filing is a standard annual report; it contains no explicit reference to Formosa\u202fPetrochemical or any outage in April\u202f2019. It merely confirms that Shell was an active downstream oil & gas producer at the time. |\n| 1    | **PetroChina** (PetroChina Co., Ltd.) | `get_supplier_info` \u2013 20\u2011F filed\u202f27\u202fApr\u202f2018 (\u2248\u202f345\u202fdays before the disruption) | The filing is a routine annual report; it does not mention Formosa\u202fPetrochemical or any supply interruption in April\u202f2019. It confirms PetroChina\u2019s status as a downstream oil & gas producer at that time. |\n| 1    | **BP** (BP plc) | `get_supplier_info` \u2013 20\u2011F filed\u202f29\u202fMar\u202f2019 (9\u202fdays before the disruption) | The filing is BP\u2019s 2018 annual report; it contains no specific discussion of Formosa\u202fPetrochemical or a supply issue in early April\u202f2019. It confirms BP was operating as a downstream oil & gas producer immediately prior to the event. |\n\n**What we can conclude from the evidence**\n\n* The three Tier\u20111 firms that have publicly filed reports were **structurally linked** to Formosa\u202fPetrochemical (they appear in the downstream graph because they either purchase refined fuels/lubricants or could serve as alternative sources).  \n* Their filings, however, do **not contain any explicit mention of the April\u202f2019 disruption at Formosa**, nor do they disclose any material impact on their operations. In other words, while they are *potentially exposed* by virtue of the supply\u2011chain relationship, there is no documentary proof (in the filings we retrieved) that the event actually affected them.\n\n**Companies for which we have no filing evidence**\n\nAll remaining downstream candidates\u2014both Tier\u202f1 and Tier\u202f2\u2014are flagged \u201cNO filing on record\u201d. Because `get_supplier_info` returns nothing for those entities, we could not retrieve any primary\u2011source documentation about them. Examples include (but are not limited to):\n\n* **Tier\u202f1 (no filing):** JSR Corp., Jiangsu Lopal Tech, Glencore, Formosa Plastics, Zeon, Sumitomo Chemical, Taiwan Cement, DL Holdings Co., Nan Ya Plastics, Formosa Chemicals & Fibre, Taita Chemical.  \n* **Tier\u202f2 (no filing):** Corteva, Dow, NIO, POSCO, Mitsui, Exxon\u202fMobil, ArcelorMittal, Nippon Steel, Apple, General Motors, China Petroleum & Chemical, Coronado Global Resources, Toyota Motor, Nexen, Alcoa, Korea Electric Power, Avient, BHP, Electra Battery Materials, etc.\n\nBecause these companies lack a filing in the database we query, **no direct evidence could be gathered** about whether they were impacted by the Formosa event. They remain *structurally at risk* according to the supply\u2011graph, but their actual exposure is unverified.\n\n**What we could not confirm**\n\n1. **Specific impact on any downstream customer or alternative supplier** \u2013 none of the filings we examined mention the April\u202f2019 disruption, nor do they disclose related financial or operational effects.  \n2. **The existence of any public disclosure (press release, news article, SEC\u2011filed \u201cmaterial event\u201d filing, etc.) linking these companies to Formosa\u2019s outage** \u2013 such a link would have required a corpus search with the company name as a filter; we have exhausted our turn budget and did not perform those searches.  \n3. **The status of many Tier\u202f1/Tier\u202f2 firms that lack filings** \u2013 without a filing or targeted corpus hit, we cannot confirm whether they were actually affected, nor can we rule out an impact.\n\n---\n\n### Bottom\u2011line summary\n\n* **Structurally at risk:** Over 50 downstream and second\u2011order companies are linked to Formosa\u202fPetrochemical, spanning oil & gas producers (Shell, PetroChina, BP, Exxon\u202fMobil, etc.), chemicals firms, steelmakers, automotive manufacturers, battery\u2011material suppliers, and technology hardware makers.  \n* **Documented evidence:** Only three of those companies have SEC\u2011type filings in our database (Shell, PetroChina, BP). Their filings are routine annual reports that *do not reference* the Formosa disruption; therefore we cannot verify any material effect on them from these sources.  \n* **Unverified exposure:** The majority of identified downstream firms either lack a filing or were not searchable within the remaining turn budget, leaving their actual impact **unconfirmed**.\n\nIn short, while the supply\u2011graph shows a broad network of potential downstream partners that could have been affected by the Formosa\u202fPetrochemical outage in early April\u202f2019, the only publicly filed documents we could retrieve do not contain any mention of that event. Consequently, we cannot substantiate concrete downstream consequences for any specific company beyond noting their structural connection to Formosa. Further investigation (e.g., targeted corpus searches or direct news\u2011source queries) would be required to determine whether any of these firms reported operational or financial impacts from the disruption.\n\nCOVERAGE GAPS (95) -- reported explicitly, not omitted:\n  - corteva: no evidence gathered\n  - dow: no evidence gathered\n  - nio: no evidence gathered\n  - posco: no evidence gathered\n  - mitsui: no evidence gathered\n  - exxon mobil: no evidence gathered\n  - arcelormittal: no evidence gathered\n  - nippon steel: no evidence gathered\n  - apple: no evidence gathered\n  - general motors: no evidence gathered\n  - china petroleum & chemical: no evidence gathered\n  - coronado global resources: no evidence gathered\n  - toyota motor: no evidence gathered\n  - nexen: no evidence gathered\n  - alcoa: no evidence gathered\n  - korea electric power: no evidence gathered\n  - avient: no evidence gathered\n  - bhp: no evidence gathered\n  - electra battery materials: no evidence gathered\n  - jsr: no evidence gathered\n  - jiangsu lopal tech: no evidence gathered\n  - glencore: no evidence gathered\n  - formosa plastics: no evidence gathered\n  - zeon: no evidence gathered\n  - sumitomo chemical: no evidence gathered\n  - taiwan cement: no evidence gathered\n  - dl holdings co lirl: no evidence gathered\n  - nan ya plastics: no evidence gathered\n  - formosa chemicals & fibre: no evidence gathered\n  - taita chemical: no evidence gathered\n  - cngr advanced material: no evidence gathered\n  - saic motor: no evidence gathered\n  - sk: no evidence gathered\n  - posco eco & challenge: no evidence gathered\n  - tong yang industry: no evidence gathered\n  - guangdong delian: no evidence gathered\n  - kumho petrochemical: no evidence gathered\n  - sumitomo electric industries: no evidence gathered\n  - shun on electronic: no evidence gathered\n  - s-oil: no evidence gathered\n  - china steel: no evidence gathered\n  - kia: no evidence gathered\n  - basf se: no evidence gathered\n  - hyundai mobis: no evidence gathered\n  - umicore: no evidence gathered\n  - saudi basic industries: no evidence gathered\n  - cia siderurgica nacional: no evidence gathered\n  - saic-gm-wuling automobile: no evidence gathered\n  - asahi kasei: no evidence gathered\n  - lg energy solution: no evidence gathered\n  - toyoda gosei: no evidence gathered\n  - samsung c&t: no evidence gathered\n  - volkswagen: no evidence gathered\n  - contemporary amperex technology: no evidence gathered\n  - guangzhou development: no evidence gathered\n  - geely automobile: no evidence gathered\n  - gem: no evidence gathered\n  - stellantis: no evidence gathered\n  - toyota tsusho: no evidence gathered\n  - nexen tire: no evidence gathered\n  - samsung sdi: no evidence gathered\n  - yokohama rubber co ltd/the: no evidence gathered\n  - e1: no evidence gathered\n  - nok: no evidence gathered\n  - byd: no evidence gathered\n  - eve energy: no evidence gathered\n  - ta yih industrial: no evidence gathered\n  - guangzhou tinci materials technology: no evidence gathered\n  - zhejiang juhua: no evidence gathered\n  - lg chem: no evidence gathered\n  - mercedes-benz: no evidence gathered\n  - astra otoparts tbk pt: no evidence gathered\n  - samsung electronics: no evidence gathered\n  - hyundai transys: no evidence gathered\n  - bridgestone: no evidence gathered\n  - sk innovation: no evidence gathered\n  - sumitomo rubber industries: no evidence gathered\n  - hyundai powertech: no evidence gathered\n  - baic motor: no evidence gathered\n  - 